consiSt("sc",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of South Carolina","d":"A Chief Justice and four associate justices, elected by the General Assembly for ten-year terms (S.C. Code § 14-3-10). Hears appeals of right directly in a short list of case types (death sentences, cases whose principal issue is the constitutionality of a state law or ordinance, public bond cases, election cases, orders limiting a State Grand Jury, minors’ abortion orders, and Public Service Commission rate cases), and otherwise reviews the Court of Appeals by discretionary certiorari (Rule 242, SCACR). Also controls bar admission, pro hac vice, lawyer discipline, the court rules, and answers certified questions from federal and other state courts (Rule 244, SCACR)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"South Carolina Court of Appeals","d":"A Chief Judge and eight associate judges, sitting in three panels of three (S.C. Code §§ 14-8-10, 14-8-80). Hears every appeal from the circuit court, family court, administrative agencies, the Administrative Law Court, and the Workers’ Compensation Commission that is not reserved to the Supreme Court (S.C. Code § 14-8-200). En banc hearing takes the vote of six judges (Rule 219, SCACR)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Court: Court of Common Pleas (civil) and Court of General Sessions (criminal)","d":"The general-jurisdiction trial court, organized in judicial circuits. Civil cases are ’common pleas’ cases governed by the SCRCP; masters-in-equity and special referees hear references from it, and their orders are appealed the same way as common pleas orders (Rule 203(b)(4), SCACR)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Family Court, Probate Court, and the Administrative Law Court","d":"Family court hears domestic relations and juvenile matters; probate court hears estates, guardianships and similar matters (direct appeal only where S.C. Code § 62-1-308(g) allows it); the Administrative Law Court hears contested agency cases, with appeals to the Court of Appeals unless a statute says otherwise (Rules 202(b), 203(b)(5)-(6), 203(d)(2), SCACR)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magistrates Courts and Municipal Courts","d":"Summary courts for small civil claims, minor criminal offenses, and preliminary matters. The SCRCP applies there only where not inconsistent with those courts’ own rules (Rule 81, SCRCP)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute in the South Carolina Code","d":"South Carolina has no anti-SLAPP law: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting tied to speech or petitioning. The suggested cite ’S.C. Code § 15-26-10 et seq.’ does not exist. Title 15 has no Chapter 26 (its chapter list jumps from Chapter 19 to Chapter 27), and the old chapter URL returns 404. Searches of the Code of Laws on scstatehouse.gov (current through the 2026 session) for ’SLAPP’, ’strategic lawsuit’, ’petition the government’, ’exercise of the right of petition’, ’participation in government’, and ’free speech in connection’ found nothing relevant.","c":"S.C. Code tit. 15 chapter index; Code of Laws searches, scstatehouse.gov (Nov. 10, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Pending bill only: S. 43 / H. 3305, ’South Carolina Public Expression Protection Act’","d":"S. 43 (2025-2026 session, sponsored by Sen. Campsen, similar to H. 3305) would add Article 7 to Chapter 3 of Title 15. It would create a procedure for civil claims based on a person’s communication in certain circumstances. It was prefiled December 11, 2024 and referred to the Senate Judiciary Committee on January 14, 2025. The bill status page shows no later action. It is not law. If it is ever enacted, its scope, deadlines, fee rules, and appeal rights must be read from the ratified act; none are described here.","c":"S. 43, 126th Gen. Assemb. (S.C. 2025-2026), bill status page","v":true},{"t":"What a speech defendant can use instead","d":"Without an anti-SLAPP statute, the tools are an ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) motion (the SC wording is failure ’to state facts sufficient to constitute a cause of action’), summary judgment under Rule 56, Rule 11 sanctions, and the Frivolous Civil Proceedings Sanctions Act. Under the Act, after a verdict or a dismissal by directed verdict, summary judgment, or JNOV, the prevailing party may move for sanctions against a frivolous claim. The test is what a reasonable attorney would believe, proof is by a preponderance, and the person accused gets notice and 30 days to respond. Unlike anti-SLAPP fee shifting, this does not end the case early and does not shift fees automatically.","c":"Rules 11, 12(b)(6), 56, SCRCP; S.C. Code § 15-36-10(A)(4), (C), (D)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: the SC lawyer files the motion; the application goes to Bar Admissions first","d":"A lawyer admitted in the highest court of another state or D.C. may be admitted pro hac vice before any South Carolina ’tribunal’: any court, the Administrative Law Court, or an agency hearing contested cases. The order of steps matters:\n1. Before any motion, submit the Application for Admission Pro Hac Vice (on the Supreme Court’s form, with a certificate of good standing) to the Supreme Court Office of Bar Admissions with a $250 fee.\n2. The Clerk of the Supreme Court certifies receipt.\n3. The associated South Carolina lawyer of record files the motion with the tribunal, attaching the application and the Clerk’s certification.\nThe tribunal may hold a hearing. If it denies the motion, it must state its reasons.","c":"Rule 404(a), (b), (d), (e), SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel is mandatory and must sign everything and attend everything","d":"No one is admitted pro hac vice unless a regular member of the South Carolina Bar in good standing is associated as attorney of record (except under Rule 244(d), which covers certified questions). The SC lawyer must at all times be prepared to go forward with the case, must sign all papers filed after admission, and must attend all later proceedings unless the tribunal specifically excuses attendance. Separately, SCRCP Rule 11(a) requires every paper to be signed by at least one attorney of record admitted to practice in South Carolina.","c":"Rule 404(a), (i), SCACR; Rule 11(a), SCRCP","v":true},{"t":"Six-application ceiling and the ’regularly engaged’ bar","d":"A lawyer who is regularly employed in South Carolina, or regularly engaged there in law practice or substantial business or professional activity, may not appear pro hac vice without applying for full admission under Rule 402. Filing more than six pro hac vice applications in a calendar year (counting Rule 404(k) arbitration and mediation statements) is treated as regularly practicing in South Carolina.","c":"Rule 404(f), SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Admission lasts through appeal; disclosures continue","d":"An admission continues through the rest of the proceeding, including appeals, unless it is withdrawn. When the case moves to another tribunal, the lawyer must immediately give the new tribunal a copy of the admission order and the name and SC Bar number of local counsel. The application discloses every SC pro hac vice application and pending appearance, and the lawyer must keep it current for as long as the appearance lasts. Permission may be withdrawn for misconduct, incomplete or false application information, pending or imposed discipline anywhere, or other good cause.","c":"Rule 404(c), (d), (g), (h), SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Arbitration and mediation in SC: a verified statement and $250 per matter","d":"Under Rule 5.5(c)(3), a lawyer licensed elsewhere may temporarily handle an arbitration, mediation, or other ADR matter in South Carolina that arises out of a pre-existing representation. For each matter, the lawyer must file a verified statement with the Office of Bar Admissions, with a $250 fee, stating that the lawyer has not filed more than three such statements in a 365-day period. The statement must be served on opposing counsel, or within 10 days after learning who opposing counsel is. More than three matters in a calendar year is presumed regular, not temporary, practice.","c":"Rule 404(j)-(l), SCACR; Rule 5.5(c)(3), RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: pro hac vice lawyers cannot e-file in the trial courts","d":"Common pleas e-filing is mandatory for represented parties in e-filing counties. Only SC-licensed lawyers in good standing may register through the Attorney Information System (AIS). The policies say lawyers admitted pro hac vice ’are not eligible’ and must file through local counsel of record. In the appellate courts, the OneDrive e-filing method is for SC-licensed lawyers, but e-mail and fax filing are open to all filers. Electronic service on the associated SC lawyer counts as service on the pro hac vice lawyer.","c":"SC Electronic Filing Policies and Guidelines §§ 2(b), 3(a)(1); S.C. Sup. Ct. Order re Rule 262 e-filing (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶¶ (b), (d)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation: allowed by the ethics rule; no court rule on limited appearances found","d":"Rule 1.2(c) lets a lawyer limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The collected SCRCP, SCACR, and e-filing texts contain no rule on ’limited scope’ or ’limited appearances’ in litigation. Once a lawyer appears of record, assume the lawyer is counsel until the court allows withdrawal. In the appellate courts, trial counsel is deemed appellate counsel until withdrawal is approved by court order (Rule 264, SCACR).","c":"Rule 1.2(c), RPC, Rule 407, SCACR; Rule 264, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Full admission: UBE score transfer, but no admission on motion in the text","d":"Rule 402 requires a UBE score of 266 or higher (from SC or any UBE jurisdiction, no more than 3 years old), an MPRE scaled score of 77 within 3 years of applying, a Course of Study on South Carolina Law, and character and fitness approval. The application fee is $1,000, plus $750 more for anyone admitted elsewhere for over a year. For SC exam-takers the fee is $1,500 if filed late in the application window. The collected Rule 402 text has no admission-on-motion route for experienced lawyers; the only exemption is for certain law deans and tenured professors (Rule 402(j)). Rule 405 separately offers a limited certificate for in-house counsel.","c":"Rule 402(c)(5), (c)(6), (c)(8), (d), (j), SCACR; Rule 405, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Newly admitted lawyers must clear Rule 403 ’trial experiences’ before appearing alone","d":"Even after admission, a South Carolina lawyer may not appear as counsel at any hearing, trial, or deposition until the Supreme Court approves the Rule 403 trial experiences. Until then, a qualified lawyer must be present throughout. A lawyer admitted in another jurisdiction for 2 years can satisfy the rule by an affidavit of equivalent experience, or a letter from a judge attesting to trial competence, filed with a $50 fee. Lawyers admitted on or before March 1, 1979 are exempt, and so are most limited-certificate holders.","c":"Rule 403(a), (e), SCACR","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: SC Rules of Professional Conduct, Rule 407, SCACR","d":"Based on the ABA Model Rules, with official comments. A pro hac vice lawyer must comply with these rules and is subject to the Supreme Court’s disciplinary jurisdiction (Rule 404(g)). The differences below are the ones the SC text itself flags or that appear on the face of the rule.","c":"Rule 407, SCACR; Rule 404(g), SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Rule 4.5: no threats of criminal or disciplinary charges to gain civil advantage","d":"A lawyer may not present, help present, or threaten to present criminal or professional disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. The comment says this rule is not in the Model Rules; it comes from former DR 7-105. Demand letters that mention prosecution or a bar complaint are a common trap for out-of-state counsel.","c":"Rule 4.5 & cmt., RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Rule 8.4: ’moral turpitude’ crimes added; no anti-harassment paragraph","d":"SC Rule 8.4(c) separately makes any criminal act involving moral turpitude professional misconduct; comment [2] notes this is a South Carolina addition. The rule’s paragraphs run (a) through (g), and (g) covers knowingly assisting a judge in misconduct. There is no separate harassment or discrimination paragraph. Biased conduct is addressed through 8.4(e) (prejudicial to the administration of justice) in comment [3].","c":"Rule 8.4 & cmts. [2]-[3], RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Fees: objective reasonableness test and ’earned on receipt’ advance fees","d":"Comment [1] says SC’s Rule 1.5(a)(2) uses an objective test rather than the Model Rule’s subjective one. Rule 1.5(f) allows an advance fee to be treated as earned immediately, and kept out of trust, only under a written fee agreement. The agreement must tell the client the nature and scope of the fee, the total amount and payment terms, that the fee will not be held in trust until earned, that the client can discharge the lawyer, and that a refund may be owed if the services are not provided.","c":"Rule 1.5(a), (f) & cmt. [1], RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Advertising and solicitation are stricter than the Model Rules","d":"Advertisements must be ’predominately informational.’ Every ad must name a responsible lawyer and give the office address and the city or town where the lawyers principally practice. Copies must be kept for 2 years. An advertised fee must be honored for 90 days, or 1 year in an annual publication. Targeted written or electronic solicitations must say ’ADVERTISING MATERIAL’ (in the e-mail subject line, too) and include two mandated disclaimer statements, one giving the SC Bar Lawyer Referral Service number. No solicitation is allowed about a personal injury, wrongful death, or accident within 30 days of the event.","c":"Rules 7.2(a), (b), (d), (g), (h), 7.3(b)(3), (c), (d), RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Smaller SC-specific provisions","d":"Rule 1.19 (not a Model Rule number) encourages written succession plans and a successor-lawyer registry kept by the SC Bar. Rule 3.5 covers ’members of the jury venire’ and allows judicial portrait funds only under listed conditions. The comment to Rule 1.16(b)(5) says the SC version expressly treats nonpayment as a ground for withdrawal.","c":"Rules 1.19, 1.16 cmt. [8], 3.5(a), (e) & cmt. [1], RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","v":true}],"style":"State of South Carolina","home":"https://www.sccourts.org/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Filing alone does not commence the action","d":"An action is commenced on filing only if the summons and complaint are served within the statute of limitations or, failing that, actually served within 120 days after filing. Serve early when limitations is close.","c":"Rule 3(a), SCRCP"},{"t":"Answers in 30 days, motions on 10 days’ notice, 5 extra days after mail service","d":"The answer is due in 30 days. A written motion and its hearing notice must be served at least 10 days before the hearing. Service by mail adds 5 days in the trial courts, but no days are added for mail in the appellate courts (Rule 263(a), SCACR).","c":"Rules 6(d), 6(e), 12(a), SCRCP; Rule 263(a), SCACR"},{"t":"Every motion needs a good-faith consultation affirmation","d":"All motions must affirm that counsel communicated with opposing counsel and tried in good faith to resolve the issue, or certify why consultation would be useless or untimely. The listed exceptions include dispositive and post-trial motions and motions against pro se parties.","c":"Rule 11(a), SCRCP"},{"t":"Discovery caps you will not see in federal court","d":"General interrogatories beyond the standard set require at least $25,000 in controversy (or declaratory, injunctive, or family court relief) and are capped at 50 including subparts. Requests for admission are capped at 20 per party. Rule 35 exams are available only when more than $100,000 in actual damages is in controversy.","c":"Rules 33(b), 35(a), 36(c), SCRCP"},{"t":"Notice of appeal: serve in 30 days from receipt of written notice of entry, then file within 10 days","d":"In civil cases, the notice of appeal must be served on all respondents within 30 days after receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment. Within 10 days after service, file it with the lower court clerk and the appellate clerk, with proof of service, the order, and the $250 fee. A late notice or an unpaid fee means dismissal. No court can extend the notice-of-appeal deadline.","c":"Rules 203(b)(1), 203(d)(1)(B), 203(d)(3), 263(b), SCACR"},{"t":"Post-trial motion deadlines changed in 2026: now 20 days","d":"An April 30, 2026 order extended the time for JNOV, Rule 52(b), and Rule 59 motions (including 59(e)) from 10 to 20 days. A timely motion stays the appeal time for all parties until receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding it. Older SC sources and practice guides still say 10 days. The amendment’s effective date is not shown in the collected text, so check it.","c":"Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), SCRCP (as amended Apr. 30, 2026); Rule 203(b)(1), SCACR"},{"t":"Rule 60(b) has no catch-all ground","d":"South Carolina’s Rule 60(b) omits the federal ’any other reason that justifies relief’ ground. Grounds (1)-(3) (mistake, new evidence, fraud) carry a hard 1-year limit.","c":"Rule 60(b), SCRCP"},{"t":"Settlements between counsel bind only if in the record or signed","d":"An agreement between counsel is enforceable only if it is a consent order or signed stipulation entered in the record, made in open court and noted on the record, or reduced to writing and signed by the parties and their counsel. A handshake or an e-mail exchange is not enough.","c":"Rule 43(k), SCRCP"},{"t":"Appeal stays civil judgments automatically (with exceptions)","d":"Unlike federal practice, serving a notice of appeal in a civil matter automatically stays the order appealed from. Money judgments, injunctions, family court orders regarding a child or support, ejectment orders, and other listed exceptions are not stayed without supersedeas. Apply to the lower court first.","c":"Rule 241(a), (b), (d)(1), SCACR"},{"t":"Rehearing must be actually received in 15 days, and it frames certiorari","d":"A petition for rehearing (limit 15 pages) must be actually received by the appellate court within 15 days after the opinion is filed; mailing it is not enough. A Court of Appeals decision cannot be reviewed by the Supreme Court until rehearing has been decided. The certiorari petition may raise only questions raised in the Court of Appeals and in the rehearing petition.","c":"Rules 221(a), 242(c), 242(d)(1), SCACR"},{"t":"Pro hac vice is not a shortcut: local counsel signs and attends, and you cannot e-file","d":"Your SC co-counsel must sign every paper and attend every proceeding unless excused. You cannot register for trial-court e-filing. More than six applications in a calendar year bars further pro hac vice appearances.","c":"Rule 404(f), (i), SCACR; SC Electronic Filing Policies and Guidelines § 3(a)(1)"},{"t":"Demand letters: no threats of prosecution or bar complaints","d":"South Carolina keeps the old Code rule: a lawyer may not threaten criminal or disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter.","c":"Rule 4.5, RPC, Rule 407, SCACR"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory change of judge","d":"None of the collected texts (SCRCP, SCACR, the e-filing policies, and the court statutes in S.C. Code Title 14, Chapters 3 and 8) provides for a peremptory challenge, notice of substitution, or automatic change of judge. The only peremptory challenges in the SCRCP are challenges to jurors (Rule 47). Disqualification must be sought for cause.","c":"SCRCP (whole text searched); Rule 47(b), SCRCP","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause under the Code of Judicial Conduct","d":"South Carolina’s Code of Judicial Conduct is Rule 501, SCACR. It still uses Canon and Section numbering, so cite Canon 3E, not Model Code Rule 2.11. A judge must step aside whenever the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including in the listed situations. The commentary says the judge should disclose on the record anything the parties might consider relevant, should tell the parties that a motion is required, and should rule on the motion before reaching substantive matters. Under Canon 3F the parties and lawyers may waive disqualification, without the judge taking part, for any ground except personal bias or prejudice concerning a party.","c":"Rule 501, SCACR, Canon 3E(1) & cmt., 3F","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court justices: statutory disqualification","d":"No justice may preside in a case in which the justice is interested, was counsel, or presided in a lower court. If justices are disqualified, the Governor commissions special justices.","c":"S.C. Code §§ 14-3-50, 14-3-60","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Cameras in court: banned by default, allowed for media on notice","d":"Broadcasting, recording, and photographing in and next to courtrooms is prohibited except as Rule 605 allows. News media may cover proceedings with video, still cameras, or recorders if they give the presiding judge reasonable notice (Form 1 if written notice is required). Without reasonable notice, the judge may refuse coverage.","c":"Rule 605(b), (f)(1), SCACR","v":true},{"t":"State constitutional speech protection: not checked","d":"The South Carolina Constitution’s free-speech and petition guarantees were not among the collected texts. Whether South Carolina courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"S.C. Const. art. I (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Expert affidavit must be filed with professional-negligence complaints","d":"In a negligence suit against a listed SC-licensed professional, including lawyers, doctors, nurses, CPAs, architects, and surveyors, the complaint must be filed with an expert’s affidavit naming at least one negligent act or omission and its factual basis. There are two exceptions: if the limitations period expires within 10 days of filing, the affidavit may follow within 45 days; and no affidavit is needed for matters of common knowledge. A defendant raises a missing or defective affidavit by a motion to dismiss filed with the first responsive pleading. A defective affidavit may be cured by amendment within 30 days. Medical malpractice claims also follow S.C. Code § 15-79-125, which was not collected.","c":"S.C. Code § 15-36-100(B)-(G)","v":true},{"t":"Secure leave (new, adopted September 23, 2026)","d":"Any lawyer admitted to practice in South Carolina may designate up to four full calendar weeks (Monday to Friday) a year as secure leave in AIS. The designation must be made at least 60 days ahead and before anything is scheduled in that week. The lawyer then cannot be required to appear at a trial, hearing, deposition, or court-annexed ADR proceeding that week. Secure leave does not extend any filing or service deadline.","c":"Rule 615, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"Statewide priority list for conflicting hearing dates","d":"When a lawyer is called to two SC tribunals at once, Rule 601 sets the priority. The order starts with the Supreme Court, Court of Appeals, and disciplinary bodies, then certain family court matters, General Sessions, Common Pleas jury terms, other family court matters, Common Pleas non-jury, the Administrative Law Court, court-annexed ADR, probate, and magistrate and municipal courts. A lawyer already in trial cannot be pulled into a higher-priority court unless the lower one grants a recess. Notify the affected tribunals as soon as the conflict appears.","c":"Rule 601, SCACR","v":true},{"t":"E-filing timing and e-service","d":"A document received by the trial-court E-Filing System by 11:59:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time is filed that day, if the clerk later accepts it. Service by Notice of Electronic Filing is complete when the document is submitted, and the policies treat it as the equivalent of mailing under Rule 5(b)(1). Whether that triggers Rule 6(e)’s added 5 days is not stated in the collected text. The appellate courts use the same 11:59:59 p.m. cutoff, and any filing fee must be mailed or delivered within five days.","c":"SC Electronic Filing Policies and Guidelines § 4(c), (e)(3); S.C. Sup. Ct. Order re Rule 262 e-filing (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶ (c)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"South Carolina Appellate Court Rules (SCACR) Part I (Rules 101-102) and Part II (Rules 201-270, with Appendix B Appeals Chart), official text from the South Carolina Judicial Branch court-rules print version generated November 10, 2026 (most recent amendments shown: April 30, 2024); Supreme Court Order re Methods of Electronic Filing and Service Under Rule 262 (as amended April 24, 2024); SCRCP Rules 50, 52, 59 (as amended April 30, 2026); S.C. Code §§ 14-3-330, 14-8-200 (scstatehouse.gov, current through the 2026 session). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in South Carolina: serve the notice within 30 days of receiving written notice of entry, and nothing extends it","d":"Most civil appeals go to the Court of Appeals. A short list goes directly to the Supreme Court, including constitutional challenges where constitutionality is the principal issue, bond cases, election cases, and death sentences. The Supreme Court then reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary certiorari. The notice of appeal is served, not just filed, and the clock runs from receipt of written notice of entry. Post-trial motions under Rules 50, 52, and 59 SCRCP stay the time for everyone. No court may extend the time to serve the notice of appeal. Briefing runs on a two-stage system: initial briefs keyed to the transcript, then a Record on Appeal, then final briefs keyed to the Record. Missing the appellant’s brief deadline produces a clerk’s dismissal order."},"groups":[["Which court",[{"t":"Court of Appeals by default","d":"Every appeal from the circuit court, family court, probate court (where direct appeal is authorized), masters and special referees, and administrative tribunals goes to the Court of Appeals unless it falls in the Supreme Court’s list. A notice filed in the wrong appellate court is transferred, not dismissed.","c":"Rules 203(d)(1)(A), 203(d)(2)(A), 204(a), SCACR; S.C. Code § 14-8-200"},{"t":"Direct to the Supreme Court","d":"File with the Clerk of the Supreme Court for:\n- a final judgment with a death sentence;\n- a final judgment where the principal issue is the constitutionality of a state law or local ordinance (the Supreme Court may transfer the case if the issue is not significant);\n- public bond and debt cases;\n- election cases;\n- orders limiting a State Grand Jury investigation;\n- family court orders on a minor’s abortion;\n- Public Service Commission utility rate decisions.","c":"Rule 203(d)(1)(A), (d)(2)(A), SCACR; S.C. Code § 14-8-200(b)"},{"t":"Certification to the Supreme Court before decision","d":"On a party’s motion, the Court of Appeals’ request, or its own motion, the Supreme Court may take a case pending in the Court of Appeals. This is normally for issues of significant public interest or major legal principles.","c":"Rule 204(b), SCACR"}]],["What can be appealed now (interlocutory orders)",[{"t":"Statutory list of appealable orders","d":"Appealable orders include final judgments and intermediate orders ’involving the merits’. They also include orders affecting a substantial right that in effect determine the action and prevent a judgment, that grant or refuse a new trial, or that strike out an answer or pleading. Final orders affecting a substantial right in special proceedings or after judgment are appealable, as are interlocutory orders granting, continuing, modifying, or refusing an injunction or a receiver. Intermediate orders not appealed earlier can be reviewed on appeal from the final judgment if they necessarily affect it.","c":"S.C. Code § 14-3-330(1)-(4); Rule 201(a), SCACR"},{"t":"Only an aggrieved party may appeal","d":"A party must be aggrieved by the order, judgment, sentence, or decision to appeal.","c":"Rule 201(b), SCACR"}]],["Notice of appeal",[{"t":"Civil (common pleas): serve within 30 days after receipt of written notice of entry","d":"The notice must be served on all respondents within 30 days after receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment. If a short or form order says a fuller order will follow, a party may wait for written notice of entry of the fuller order. The same rule governs domestic relations appeals from family court, appeals from masters and special referees, and authorized direct probate appeals.","c":"Rule 203(b)(1), (3), (4), (5), SCACR"},{"t":"Post-trial motions stay the time for all parties","d":"A timely motion for JNOV (Rule 50), to alter or amend (Rules 52 and 59), or for a new trial (Rule 59) stays the appeal time for all parties. The time runs from receipt of written notice of entry of the order granting or denying the motion. As of the April 30, 2026 SCRCP amendments, those motions are due within 20 days (formerly 10).","c":"Rule 203(b)(1), SCACR; Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), SCRCP"},{"t":"Then file within 10 days after service, with the fee","d":"File the notice with the lower court clerk and the appellate clerk within 10 days after service. Attach proof of service, a copy of the written order(s) appealed, and the $250 filing fee (set by order of October 9, 2018; not charged in criminal appeals or to the State). If the notice is not filed on time or the fee is not paid in full, the appeal is dismissed and can be reinstated only under Rule 260.","c":"Rule 203(d)(1)(B), (d)(3) & n.1, SCACR"},{"t":"Administrative tribunal appeals: 30 days from receipt of the decision","d":"Serve the agency, the Administrative Law Court (if involved), and all parties of record within 30 days after receipt of the decision. File with the appellate clerk within the same 30 days. A timely petition for rehearing to the tribunal stays the time.","c":"Rule 203(b)(6), (d)(2)(B), SCACR"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"A respondent cross-appeals by serving a notice of appeal within 5 days after receiving the appellant’s notice, or within the regular Rule 203(b) time, whichever expires later.","c":"Rule 203(c), SCACR"},{"t":"Contents and form","d":"The notice names the court, judge, and county, the lower court docket number and any related appeals, the date of the order and (where timeliness depends on it) when notice of it was received, the appealing party, and every attorney of record with contact details. Use the Appendix form. Appeals from guilty pleas need a written explanation of the reviewable issue.","c":"Rule 203(d)(1)(B)(iv), (e), SCACR"},{"t":"The appellate court takes exclusive jurisdiction on service","d":"Once the notice is served, the appellate court has exclusive jurisdiction over the appeal. The lower court keeps power over supersedeas petitions and matters not affected by the appeal.","c":"Rule 205, SCACR"}]],["Stays and supersedeas",[{"t":"Automatic stay in civil matters","d":"Serving a notice of appeal in a civil matter automatically stays the matters decided and the relief ordered, for the whole appeal, unless the stay is lifted.","c":"Rule 241(a), SCACR"},{"t":"Exceptions (no automatic stay)","d":"The exceptions include money judgments (S.C. Code § 18-9-130), judgments directing delivery of documents or property or the execution of conveyances, sales or delivery of real property, perishable property, family court orders regarding a child or support, workers’ compensation awards, injunctions and TROs, family court temporary fee awards, ejectment orders, and certain administrative appeals. The list is not complete, and conditions for an exception must be strictly met.","c":"Rule 241(b), SCACR"},{"t":"Procedure","d":"Apply first to the lower court or tribunal unless extraordinary circumstances make that impracticable. Then petition the appellate court with a petition verified by the client. Attach a certified copy of the order and the notice of appeal with proof of service. Relief may be conditioned on a bond.","c":"Rule 241(c)(3), (d), SCACR"}]],["Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal",[{"t":"Order the transcript in 10 days (civil)","d":"In civil and domestic appeals, arrange the transcript in writing with the court reporter within 10 days after serving the notice of appeal (30 days in criminal and juvenile cases). Copy all parties, Court Administration, and the appellate clerk. The reporter has 60 days to deliver, and Court Administration may grant up to three extensions totaling 90 days. The whole transcript must be ordered unless the parties agree in writing otherwise.","c":"Rule 207(a)(1)-(3), SCACR"},{"t":"Initial briefs: 30 / 30 / 10 days","d":"The appellant’s initial brief is due 30 days after receiving the transcript (or 30 days after serving the notice of appeal if no transcript). The respondent’s brief is due 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief, and an optional reply 10 days after service of the respondent’s brief. If the appellant misses the deadline, the clerk signs a dismissal order.","c":"Rule 208(a), SCACR"},{"t":"Designation of the record served with each brief","d":"Each party serves, with its initial brief (including a reply), a Designation of Matter to be Included in the Record on Appeal. Signing it certifies that nothing irrelevant is included.","c":"Rule 209, SCACR"},{"t":"Record on Appeal: 30 days after the last brief","d":"The appellant serves the Record on Appeal within 30 days after service of the last brief. It must be filed no later than the final briefs. The court will not consider facts outside the Record.","c":"Rule 210(a), (b), (h), SCACR"},{"t":"Final briefs: 20 days after service of the Record","d":"Final briefs must be identical to the initial briefs except for record citations, changed to ’(R. p. _, line _)’ form, and correction of obvious typographical errors.","c":"Rule 211, SCACR"},{"t":"Brief contents","d":"The brief must contain:\n- tables of contents and authorities;\n- a statement of issues on appeal (points not stated ordinarily will not be considered);\n- a statement of the case, which binds the appellant and must not contain contested matters;\n- a ’Standard of Review’ section;\n- argument by issue;\n- a short conclusion.\nThe respondent may argue for affirmance on any ground in the record.","c":"Rules 208(b)(1)-(2), 220(c), SCACR"}]],["Length and format limits",[{"t":"Page limits (not word counts)","d":"Principal briefs are limited to 50 pages and reply briefs to 25 (no limit in death-penalty cases); exceeding them requires a motion. Other limits: a petition for rehearing, 15 pages; a petition for certiorari to the Court of Appeals, 25 pages; the return, 25; the reply, 15.","c":"Rules 208(b)(5), 221(a), 242(d)(3), (f), (g), SCACR"},{"t":"Typeface and paper","d":"Use 12-point or larger type, double spaced, with one-inch margins. Initial briefs are stapled at the top left. Electronic filings must be PDF.","c":"Rules 208(b)(8), 267, SCACR; Order re Rule 262 (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶ (b)"}]],["Motions, time, and dismissal",[{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Every motion needs a certificate of service, a supporting memorandum, and supporting affidavits where facts are outside the record. Returns are due in 10 days and replies in 5. The motion fee is $50, with listed exceptions. Filing a motion does not stay briefing deadlines, except a motion to dismiss or to be relieved as counsel. A single judge or justice may rule, and review is by petition for rehearing.","c":"Rule 240(b)-(f), (j), SCACR"},{"t":"Counting time: no extra days for mail","d":"Exclude the trigger day. If the last day falls on a weekend or holiday, the period runs to the next business day. Periods under 7 days exclude weekends and holidays. Unlike the trial courts, no days are added for service by mail; SCRCP Rule 6(e) does not apply. The appellate court may extend any deadline except the time to serve a notice of appeal. Parties cannot extend deadlines by agreement.","c":"Rule 263, SCACR"},{"t":"Dismissal and reinstatement","d":"If the appellant misses a rule requirement, the clerk issues a dismissal order. To reinstate, a motion showing good cause must be actually received within 15 days after the dismissal order is filed, or the case is remitted.","c":"Rule 260(a), SCACR"},{"t":"Frivolous appeals","d":"The court may sanction attorneys or parties for frivolous appeals, petitions, motions, or returns, or for rule violations, after 10 days’ notice.","c":"Rule 269, SCACR"}]],["Rehearing, remittitur, and costs",[{"t":"Rehearing: actually received within 15 days","d":"A petition for rehearing must be actually received within 15 days after the opinion is filed. It must state with particularity what was overlooked or misapprehended, and is limited to 15 pages. No return is allowed unless the court requests one, and rehearing is ordinarily not granted without a request. No rehearing is allowed from denial of certiorari, refusal of original jurisdiction, or denial of reinstatement.","c":"Rule 221(a), SCACR"},{"t":"En banc in the Court of Appeals","d":"A suggestion for en banc rehearing goes inside the petition for rehearing. Six judges must vote for it.","c":"Rule 219, SCACR"},{"t":"Remittitur","d":"The remittitur is sent no sooner than 15 days after the decision. It is held while a rehearing petition is pending and, in the Court of Appeals, until the time for certiorari runs out.","c":"Rule 221(b), SCACR"},{"t":"Costs and the fixed attorney’s fee","d":"In civil appeals the prevailing party may tax:\n- the filing fee;\n- transcript costs;\n- bond premiums;\n- printing costs;\n- an attorney’s fee set by the Supreme Court at $2,500 (order of January 17, 2018).\nMove for costs, with a sworn itemized statement, within 15 days after the remittitur issues.","c":"Rule 222(b), (d) & n.1, SCACR"}]],["Review in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Rehearing in the Court of Appeals comes first","d":"A Court of Appeals decision is not final for Supreme Court review until the Court of Appeals rules on a petition for rehearing or reinstatement. Only questions raised in the Court of Appeals and in the rehearing petition may be presented.","c":"Rule 242(c), (d)(1), SCACR"},{"t":"Certiorari: 30 days after rehearing is decided","d":"Serve the petition and file it with both clerks within 30 days after the Court of Appeals finally decides rehearing, with a $250 fee. Returns are due in 30 days and replies in 10. Review is discretionary; listed factors include novel questions, a dissent below, conflict with Supreme Court precedent, substantial constitutional issues, and conflict with the U.S. Supreme Court on a federal question. Since April 30, 2024 the Appendix requirement is suspended; the Court uses the electronic record.","c":"Rule 242(b), (c), (e) n.2, (f), (g), SCACR"},{"t":"If certiorari is granted","d":"Briefing is 30 / 30 / 10 days from the grant, and there is no oral argument unless ordered.","c":"Rule 242(i), SCACR"},{"t":"Certified questions and original jurisdiction","d":"The Supreme Court may answer questions of SC law certified by a federal court or another state’s appellate court. Out-of-state counsel must move for pro hac vice within 15 days after the order accepting the question. Original-jurisdiction petitions must show why the matter cannot first be decided in a lower court.","c":"Rules 244(a), (d), 245(a), SCACR"}]],["Filing and service (including electronic)",[{"t":"Filing methods and the mailbox rule","d":"Documents may be filed by hand delivery, by first-class mail (filed on the mailing date), or electronically as the Supreme Court orders. The mailbox rule does not apply to petitions for rehearing or motions to reinstate, which must be actually received. Every filing needs proof of service.","c":"Rule 262(a), (b), SCACR"},{"t":"Electronic filing","d":"SC-licensed lawyers may file through OneDrive for Business (registration through AIS, ’Appellate Filings’). Anyone may file by e-mail to supctfilings@sccourts.org or ctappfilings@sccourts.org (PDF only, no file-sharing links) or by fax. A document received by 11:59:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time is filed that day. Any fee must be mailed or delivered within five days.","c":"Order re Rule 262 (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶¶ (b), (c)"},{"t":"Electronic service","d":"SC lawyers may serve each other at the primary e-mail address in AIS (PDF attachment), and must include a copy of the sent e-mail with the proof of service. Service on the associated SC lawyer counts as service on a pro hac vice lawyer. A notice of appeal may be served through trial-court e-filing, with the Notice of Electronic Filing as proof.","c":"Rule 262(c)(3), SCACR; Order re Rule 262 (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶ (d)"},{"t":"Counsel continue on appeal","d":"Trial counsel and guardians ad litem remain counsel on appeal until the appellate court approves withdrawal by written order.","c":"Rule 264, SCACR"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Serve notice of appeal (civil, common pleas, domestic family court, masters, probate)","d":"30 days from receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment.\nCannot be extended by any court (Rule 263(b)). Restarts from receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding a timely Rule 50, 52 or 59 motion.","c":"Rule 203(b)(1), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended by any court (Rule 263(b)). Restarts from receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding a timely Rule 50, 52 or 59 motion."}},{"t":"File the served notice of appeal with lower court and appellate clerks","d":"10 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nWith proof of service, copy of order, and $250 fee; failure means dismissal (Rule 203(d)(3)).","c":"Rule 203(d)(1)(B), SCACR","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal)","note":"With proof of service, copy of order, and $250 fee; failure means dismissal (Rule 203(d)(3))."}},{"t":"Serve and file notice of appeal from an administrative tribunal","d":"30 days from receipt of the decision.\nA timely petition for rehearing to the tribunal stays the time.","c":"Rule 203(b)(6), (d)(2)(B), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of the decision)","note":"A timely petition for rehearing to the tribunal stays the time."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"5 days from receipt of appellant’s notice of appeal.\nOr the regular Rule 203(b) time, whichever expires later.","c":"Rule 203(c), SCACR","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of appellant’s notice of appeal)","note":"Or the regular Rule 203(b) time, whichever expires later."}},{"t":"Post-trial motions that stay appeal time (JNOV, 52(b), 59 new trial, 59(e))","d":"20 days from jury discharge (jury cases) or receipt of written notice of entry (non-jury and 59(e)).\nExtended from 10 days by the April 30, 2026 amendments; effective date not shown in collected text.","c":"Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), SCRCP","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (jury discharge)","note":"Extended from 10 days by the April 30, 2026 amendments; effective date not shown in collected text."}},{"t":"Order transcript (civil)","d":"10 days from service of notice of appeal.\n30 days in General Sessions and juvenile appeals.","c":"Rule 207(a)(1), SCACR","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of notice of appeal)","note":"30 days in General Sessions and juvenile appeals."}},{"t":"Appellant’s initial brief","d":"30 days from receipt of transcript (or service of notice of appeal if none).\nMissing it produces a clerk’s dismissal order.","c":"Rule 208(a)(1), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of transcript)","note":"Missing it produces a clerk’s dismissal order."}},{"t":"Respondent’s initial brief","d":"30 days from service of appellant’s brief.","c":"Rule 208(a)(2), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"10 days from service of respondent’s brief.\nOptional.","c":"Rule 208(a)(3), SCACR","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of respondent’s brief)","note":"Optional."}},{"t":"Serve Record on Appeal","d":"30 days from service of the last brief.\nFile no later than final briefs.","c":"Rule 210(a), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the last brief)","note":"File no later than final briefs."}},{"t":"Final briefs","d":"20 days from service of the Record on Appeal.","c":"Rule 211(a), SCACR","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the Record on Appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Return to motion / reply","d":"10 days from service of motion (reply: 5 days from service of return).","c":"Rule 240(e), (f), SCACR","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"15 days from filing of the opinion, order, judgment, or decree.\nMust be actually received; 15-page limit.","c":"Rule 221(a), SCACR","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion, order, judgment, or decree)","note":"Must be actually received; 15-page limit."}},{"t":"Motion to reinstate after clerk’s dismissal","d":"15 days from filing of the dismissal order.\nMust be actually received.","c":"Rule 260(a), SCACR","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the dismissal order)","note":"Must be actually received."}},{"t":"Petition for certiorari to review Court of Appeals","d":"30 days from final decision on petition for rehearing or reinstatement in the Court of Appeals.\n$250 fee; 25-page limit.","c":"Rule 242(c), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (final decision on petition for rehearing or reinstatement in the Court of Appeals)","note":"$250 fee; 25-page limit."}},{"t":"Return to certiorari petition","d":"30 days from service of the petition.\nReply due 10 days after service of return.","c":"Rule 242(f), SCACR","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":"Reply due 10 days after service of return."}},{"t":"Motion for costs","d":"15 days from issuance of the remittitur.\nSworn itemized statement required.","c":"Rules 222(d), 242(j)(4), SCACR","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the remittitur)","note":"Sworn itemized statement required."}},{"t":"Motion to argue against precedent orally","d":"15 days from before oral argument.\nCounted backward; no leave needed to argue against precedent in the brief.","c":"Rule 217, SCACR","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (before oral argument)","note":"Counted backward; no leave needed to argue against precedent in the brief."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.sccourts.org/"]]},"rulesName":"South Carolina Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of Rules","s":"The rules govern procedure in all South Carolina courts in every civil suit, whether at law or in equity, subject to the exceptions in Rule 81, and are construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"2","t":"One Form of Action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action”; the Note says this abolishes the largely cosmetic distinction between actions and special proceedings (such as mandamus or habeas corpus).","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of Action","s":"An action is commenced when the summons and complaint are filed with the clerk, but only if they are served within the statute of limitations or, if not, actual service is made no later than 120 days after filing (unlike the federal rule, commencement turns on service as well as filing). A plaintiff may move to proceed in forma pauperis with the proposed complaint and an affidavit of inability to pay; inability is presumed when net household income is at or below the federal Poverty Guidelines, and filing fees are waived without a motion for parties represented through qualifying legal aid, legal services, or SC Bar Pro Bono programs upon the attorney’s written certification.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The plaintiff or plaintiff’s attorney (not the clerk, unlike the federal rule) issues the summons; service may be made by the sheriff, a deputy, or any non-party, non-attorney at least 18 years old, and summons and complaint must be served together. Methods include personal or abode service, service on agents, officers of entities, the Attorney General (with registered or certified mail) for the State, statutory service, registered or certified mail with return receipt and restricted delivery (effective on the delivery date; no default without a receipt showing the defendant’s acceptance), and qualifying commercial delivery services; minors under 14 and incompetents are served personally plus a guardian or caretaker, and parents of minors 14 to 18 are also served. Proof of service is by sheriff’s certificate or affidavit, failure to prove service does not void it, process may be amended absent material prejudice, and written acceptance of service needs no further proof.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Service of Process in Foreign Countries","s":"Adopted in 2022 from the federal rule: individuals abroad may be served by internationally agreed means such as the Hague Service Convention, by methods allowed by the foreign country’s law or a letter rogatory, by personal delivery or clerk-sent signed-receipt mail unless foreign law prohibits it, or by other court-ordered means; entities may be served the same ways except personal delivery. Proof follows the treaty or a signed receipt or other satisfactory evidence, failure of proof does not affect validity, and amendment and written acceptance rules mirror Rule 4.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and Filing of Pleadings and Other Papers","s":"Orders, later pleadings, written motions, discovery requests and responses, notices, offers of judgment, and similar papers must be served on every party of record (parties in default need service only of new claims, served as under Rule 4, and notice of any hearing on unliquidated damages); service on a represented party is made on the attorney by delivery or mail, and mail service is complete on mailing. Civil process may be served on Sunday except on a person going to, from, or attending a church or religious service, and proposed orders given to the court must be served on all counsel at the same time and by the same means. Papers must be filed within 5 days after service, the summons and complaint must be filed before service, and proof of service of the summons and complaint must be filed within 10 days after service; the court may dismiss on its own initiative for failure to serve.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or State or Federal holiday, and exclude intermediate weekends and holidays when the period is under 7 days. Counsel may extend a deadline once by written agreement for up to the original period, and the court may enlarge time before expiration or for good cause after it, but not for Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59, and 60(b), and the time for notice of intent to appeal is jurisdictional. Motions and hearing notices must be served at least 10 days before the hearing (the Note says the federal rule uses 5), opposing affidavits at least 2 days before, a party has 20 days to comply with a payment or condition unless ordered otherwise, and 5 days are added after service by mail or on a statutory agent (the Note says the federal rule adds 3).","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings Allowed; Form of Motions","s":"The pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, and third-party complaint and answer, plus a court-ordered reply and an optional reply to affirmative defenses under Rule 8(c). Motions must be in writing, state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought unless made in open court with a court reporter present; demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency are abolished.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"8","t":"General Rules of Pleading","s":"Unlike the federal rule, South Carolina keeps fact pleading: a claim must state the jurisdictional grounds, the facts showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for judgment, and punitive damages may be demanded only in general terms, not a stated sum, though a party may cap the total amount in controversy. Denials must fairly meet each averment (a general denial only if the pleader in good faith contests everything), the list of affirmative defenses is longer than the federal list and includes condonation, recrimination, and plene administravit, a reply to any affirmative defense is allowed, and averments not denied are admitted except as to the amount of damages.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading Special Matters","s":"Capacity need not be pleaded but must be challenged by specific negative averment; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while conditions of mind and performance of conditions precedent may be averred generally. Special damages must be specifically stated, libel or slander may be pleaded generally as published concerning the pleader with truth and mitigation allowed in the answer, and an action on an account requires a verified copy of the account or a verified pleading.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of Pleadings","s":"Pleadings need a caption with State, county, court, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; unknown adverse parties may be named with “whose true name is unknown”; averments go in numbered paragraphs with each cause of action and defense stated separately, and exhibits are part of a pleading only if a copy is attached. Papers must be 8½ by 11 inches with at least 1½ line spacing, 1-inch margins, and 12-point type, numbered and fastened at the top, and the clerk must refuse papers that do not comply.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of Pleadings; Attorneys","s":"Every paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record admitted in South Carolina, with address and telephone number (or by the unrepresented party), and the signature certifies the paper was read, has good ground, and is not interposed for delay; the Note says SC kept a standard closer to the pre-1983 federal rule. Every motion must affirm that counsel consulted opposing counsel in good faith first, except motions to dismiss, for summary judgment, new trial, or JNOV, Family Court temporary relief, foreclosures, or pro se opponents; the court may sanction violations with expenses and attorney’s fees. Attorneys may be changed only by consent or for cause by court order, affidavits and verifications follow set forms, and no attorney may act as surety on a bond.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and Objections","s":"An answer is due within 30 days after service of the complaint (the Note says the federal rule allows 20), as are answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims; the State answers post-conviction relief applications within 60 days for guilty pleas and 90 days for trials. After a Rule 12 motion is denied or postponed, or after a more definite statement or ruling on a motion to strike, the responsive pleading is due within 15 days; a more definite statement must be provided within 15 days, and a motion to strike is due before responding or within 30 days if no response is required. Defenses that may be raised by motion include the federal ones (with “failure to state facts sufficient to constitute a cause of action”) plus another action pending between the same parties for the same claim, which is waived like personal jurisdiction, venue, and process defenses if not timely raised; outside matters convert a 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion into summary judgment.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and Cross-Claim","s":"A pleading must state any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence (compulsory, barred if omitted), may state any other claim (permissive), and may state cross-claims against co-parties arising from the same transaction, occurrence, or property; later-maturing claims come in by supplemental pleading and omitted ones by leave of court. Unique to South Carolina, Rule 13(j) transfers an action from a court of limited jurisdiction to circuit court when a counterclaim or cross-claim exceeds that court’s jurisdiction, with transfer discretionary for permissive counterclaims, which are not barred later if not transferred.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-Party Practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the claim, without leave if filed no later than 10 days after serving the original answer and otherwise on motion; a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may do the same. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 14(c) lets the court order a third-party defendant joined as a regular plaintiff or defendant under Rules 19 or 20, after which the “third party” label is dropped.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and Supplemental Pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of course before or within 30 days after a responsive pleading is served, or within 30 days after service if no response is required and the case is not on the trial roster (the Note says the federal period was 20); otherwise amendment requires leave, freely given when justice requires and no party is prejudiced, or written consent. A response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining original time or 15 days after service, whichever is longer; amendments to conform to the evidence require the court to state its reasons on the record and grant any needed continuance, relation back follows the federal standard, and supplemental pleadings require a motion.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"16","t":"Pre-Trial Procedure; Formulating Issues","s":"After issues are joined the court may hold a pre-trial hearing on matters such as simplifying issues, limiting expert witnesses and discovery time, and referring issues to a master, and counsel must come prepared to state provable facts and law, itemize damages, provide medical records, make good-faith settlement offers, and exchange witness and exhibit lists. A written pre-trial order controls the case and may exclude unlisted witnesses or exhibits, deem unpresented motions abandoned, and address electronically stored information and privilege clawback. Counsel must give the judge a uniform pre-trial brief (served on all parties at the same time and by the same means, and never filed), the circuit’s Chief Administrative Judge may keep a pre-trial calendar, and the trial judge may hold informal status conferences; the Note says this is a “hearing” producing an order rather than the federal “conference.”","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties Plaintiff and Defendant; Capacity","s":"Actions are brought by the real party in interest, with a reasonable time after objection to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal, and capacity is governed by South Carolina law. Representatives sue for minors and incompetents, otherwise a next friend or guardian ad litem, and persons imprisoned outside South Carolina appear by guardian ad litem; Rule 17(d), which is not in the federal rule, sets who may appoint and serve as guardian ad litem, lets a minor 14 or older apply personally, and lets any party apply if no application is made within 30 days after service of the summons. The rule also permits suing unknown owners, heirs, or devisees in property actions and requires the legal representative and all tenants in common to be parties in partition of a decedent’s real estate, with limited exceptions.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of Claims and Remedies","s":"A party may join as many legal or equitable claims as it has against an opposing party, and claims that formerly required a prior judgment (such as a money claim and setting aside a fraudulent conveyance) may be joined. Unlike federal Rule 18, Rule 18(c) expressly lets the court order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or unnecessary expense.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of Persons Needed for Just Adjudication","s":"Same as the federal rule: a person must be joined if feasible when complete relief requires it or the person’s absence risks impairing that person’s interest or exposing parties to inconsistent obligations, and if joinder is not feasible the court weighs prejudice, ability to shape relief, adequacy of judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy in deciding whether to proceed or dismiss. A pleader must name nonjoined required persons and explain why, and the rule is subject to Rule 23.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive Joinder of Parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; parties need not be interested in all the relief. The court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and Non-Joinder of Parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties at any stage on motion or its own initiative on just terms, and may sever any claim.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants against a plaintiff may be joined and required to interplead when the plaintiff faces double or multiple liability, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability, and a defendant may seek interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim; the federal statutory-interpleader provision is omitted. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 22(b) lets the party seeking interpleader deposit the money or property with the court or post a bond and be discharged from liability while the claimants litigate.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"23","t":"Class Actions","s":"A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequate representation, and, unless the primary relief is injunctive or declaratory for the class, more than $100 in controversy for each class member (a requirement the Note says is not in the federal rule). The rule also covers shareholder derivative actions (verified complaint, demand allegations) and unincorporated-association actions, requires court approval and notice to members for any dismissal or compromise, and directs the court to decide class status as soon as practicable. Unique to South Carolina, at least 50% of residual class funds left after the claims process must go to the South Carolina Bar Foundation for civil legal access for low-income residents, with the balance to that foundation or related purposes.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Same as the federal rule: intervention of right on timely application where a statute grants an unconditional right or the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and permissive intervention for a conditional statutory right, a common question, or a government officer or agency administering a relevant statute or regulation. The motion must be served under Rule 5 with a pleading attached, and when the constitutionality of a statute is questioned and the State is not a party, the motion must also be served on the Attorney General.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of Parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, any party or the decedent’s successor may move for substitution, served under Rule 5 on parties and under Rule 4 on nonparties; the action may be dismissed if substitution is not made within a reasonable time, which the Note says replaces the federal 90-day limit, and the decedent’s counsel must promptly notify other parties of the death and the proper substitutes. Actions do not abate after judgment, and the rule also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, automatic substitution of successor public officers, and (unlike the federal rule) substitution at any stage, including by the appellate court pending appeal.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"26","t":"General Provisions Governing Discovery","s":"Discovery extends to any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter, including insurance agreements, with work-product and expert protections; the court must limit discovery that is cumulative, already available, or unduly burdensome or disproportionate, including electronically stored information that is not reasonably accessible. Unlike the federal rule, a testifying expert may be discovered by any method without a special showing, informally consulted experts need not be disclosed, and a party retaining an expert must produce the expert in South Carolina for deposition at the requesting party’s expense for a reasonable fee; a 2024 amendment adds federal-style protection for attorney-expert communications and draft reports. Requests under Rules 31, 33, 34, and 36 are continuing until trial, objections to a discovery-conference motion are due within 10 days after service of the motion, discovery is served but not filed until used, and non-deposition discovery may be destroyed 1 year after final termination; the Note says the stricter federal Rule 11, 16, and 26 certification standard was not adopted.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions Before Action or Pending Appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party but presently unable to sue may file a verified petition in the circuit where an expected adverse party resides to perpetuate testimony, serving notice at least 20 days before the hearing as Rule 4(d) provides for summons (or as the court orders, with an appointed attorney for those not served); the court may order the depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice. The trial court may also allow depositions to perpetuate testimony while an appeal is pending or before the appeal time expires; the Note says this is not a pre-suit discovery device.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons Before Whom Depositions May Be Taken; Depositions in Out-of-State Actions","s":"Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court appointee, and abroad on notice, by commission, or by letter rogatory; no deposition may be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or counsel, or anyone financially interested. Unique to South Carolina, Rule 28(d) lets a South Carolina-licensed attorney or the clerk issue a subpoena for a deposition or document production in South Carolina for an out-of-state case after filing the foreign mandate, commission, notice, or agreement and paying the filing fee set by administrative rule (a footnote says it was set at $50 effective March 1, 2003); the witness may be compelled to attend only in the county of residence, employment, or in-person business.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations Regarding Discovery Procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and may modify other discovery procedures only to the extent Rule 6(b) allows; the 2001 Note says written agreement allows only one extension, for no more than the original time, without court approval.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions Upon Oral Examination","s":"Leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service on any defendant (unless the defendant has sought discovery or the deponent is about to travel more than 100 miles away, leave the State, or go to sea) and for prisoners; written notice must be given 10 days in advance (the Note says the federal rule requires only reasonable notice). Unlike the federal rule, a person may be deposed only once absent agreement or good cause, witnesses are deposed in the county of residence, employment, or business (parties also where the case is pending), witnesses get $25 per day plus state-rate mileage, these limits do not apply where less than $10,000 is in controversy unless agreed or ordered, and the text sets no time limit on a deposition’s length. Other SC provisions: the deponent signs within 30 days or the officer signs, originals may be destroyed 1 year after final termination, detailed videotape rules apply in cases seeking $100,000 or more or by court order, treating health-care providers’ evidence depositions are usable at trial (the noticing party must give 2 days’ notice of intent to call the provider live), and conduct rules bar speaking objections, instructions not to answer except for privilege, court limits, or a Rule 30(d) motion (which must be filed within 5 business days), and private substantive conferences, with documents provided at least 2 business days ahead removing the right to confer about them.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions Upon Written Questions","s":"Any party may depose any person by written questions after the action begins, serving the questions with a notice naming the deponent and officer (prisoners only by leave); cross questions are due within 30 days after service, redirect within 10 days after cross, and recross within 10 days after redirect, and the officer takes and certifies the answers as under Rule 30. The Note says this device had no counterpart in prior State practice.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of Depositions in Court Proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of trial or out of the State (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend, not obtainable by subpoena, or in exceptional circumstances; depositions from a prior action on the same subject between the same parties may also be used. Unique to South Carolina, at least 1 day before offering deposition excerpts in the case in chief, counsel must give the judge and all counsel the excerpts by page and line and the exhibits, and the opponent must furnish objections and counter-excerpts when they are offered (not required for impeachment). Objections to form of Rule 31 written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round of questions and within 5 days after the last questions, and most procedural irregularities are waived unless promptly raised.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to Parties","s":"Interrogatories may be served on any party (the Note says not just an adverse party), and answers or objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Unique to South Carolina, standard interrogatories (witnesses and statements, documents and photographs, treating physicians and medical costs in injury cases, liability insurance, itemized damages excluding pain and suffering, trial experts, witness fact summaries, and for defendants correct identification) may be served in every case and are continuing until trial. Additional general interrogatories are allowed by right only where at least $25,000 is in controversy, in declaratory or injunctive actions, or in family court, capped at 50 questions including subparts per party absent leave for good cause; the court may allow more in any case for good cause.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of Documents and Things and Entry Upon Land","s":"A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry onto land, describing items with reasonable particularity and optionally specifying the form for ESI. The written response is due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; documents must be produced as kept or labeled to the categories, ESI in its ordinary or a reasonably usable form and only once, and nonparties are reached only under Rule 45.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and Mental Examinations","s":"Unlike the federal rule, an examination may be ordered only where more than $100,000 in actual damages is in controversy, on motion for good cause, when a party’s mental or physical condition (including blood group) is in controversy. The examined party’s own physician may attend, and unless agreed or ordered for good cause the exam is held in the county where the examinee or the examinee’s physician resides; the court may designate the physician if the parties cannot agree, but that designation is inadmissible at trial. On request the examined party gets a detailed report, the examining party then gets reports of other exams of the same condition, and obtaining the report waives privilege as to others who examined that condition.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"36","t":"Request for Admission","s":"A party may request admission of facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents; a matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service (or as the court allows or the parties stipulate under Rules 29 and 6(b)), but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion, and unlike the federal rule, Rule 36(c) caps requests at 20 per party including subparts, absent leave for good cause, with no limit on requests to admit the genuineness of documents or things.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to Make or Cooperate in Discovery; Sanctions","s":"A party may move to compel answers, designations, or inspection, an evasive or incomplete answer counts as a failure, and the losing side ordinarily pays reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees unless substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order allows established facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, contempt (except for refusing an exam), and expenses, and the same sanctions (except contempt) apply to a complete failure to attend one’s deposition or respond to interrogatories or inspection requests; costs of proving matters not admitted are awarded with listed exceptions. Unlike the federal rule, the Note says a motion concerning a party’s deposition may be brought only in the court where the action is pending, and absent exceptional circumstances no sanction applies for ESI lost through routine good-faith operation of an electronic system.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury Trial of Right","s":"The constitutional and statutory right to a jury is preserved, and fact issues in an action for money only or for specific real or personal property must be tried by a jury unless waived. A written jury demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue, another party may demand other issues within 10 days after a partial demand, failure to serve and file the demand waives a jury, and a demand may not be withdrawn without consent except against a party in default.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by Jury or by the Court","s":"Demanded issues are tried by jury unless the parties stipulate in writing or on the record to a bench trial or the court finds no jury right exists; other issues are tried by the court or referred to a master under Rule 53, though the court may order a jury despite a missing demand. In non-jury actions the court may use an advisory jury, or with both parties’ consent a jury whose verdict has the same effect as one of right.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"40","t":"General Docket, Trial Rosters, and Call of Cases for Trial","s":"Unique to South Carolina (the federal rule leaves calendaring to local rules): the clerk keeps a General Docket, a Jury Trial Roster, and a Nonjury Docket; filings must state jury or nonjury (undesignated cases go nonjury), and discovery, scheduling, and emergency motions are captioned “Priority Matter” and heard first. A case is tried no earlier than 30 days after it reaches the Jury Trial Roster and is called in roster order; the first 20 cases at the opening of a term (plus 20 for each additional judge) may be called at once and others on at least 24 hours’ notice, and no case may be called until 180 days after service of the last pleading adding a new party, or a contested nonjury action until 120 days after filing or the last pleading adding a party, absent written consent. Transfer to the jury roster is by agreement at any time; on request no earlier than 180 days after filing (responses due within 10 days, with objectors stating a consent date within 9 months); on request from 9 months (with a mandatory scheduling order on request, responses within 10 days); automatically after 12 months on the General Docket; or on a ready-for-trial request no earlier than 120 days after filing heard by the Chief Judge for Administrative Purposes; continuances are ordinarily only to the next term (each calendar week is a term) and for an absent witness require a sworn showing; a party may strike its claim once by written agreement, tolling limitations if restored on motion within 1 year with at least 10 days’ notice.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of Actions; Non-Suit","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing and serving notice before the adverse party serves an answer or summary judgment motion (the Note says SC requires service as well as filing) or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; dismissal is without prejudice unless stated, except a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order, not over objection if a pending counterclaim cannot stand alone; a defendant may move to dismiss for failure to prosecute or comply, or for no right to relief after the plaintiff’s evidence in a bench trial, and such involuntary dismissals operate on the merits unless for jurisdiction, venue, or Rule 19 joinder. The rule applies to counterclaims, cross-claims, and third-party claims, and costs of a prior dismissed action may be ordered.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"41.1","t":"Sealing Documents and Settlement Agreements","s":"Unique to South Carolina and grounded in the State constitution’s open-courts guarantee: absent another governing rule, a party must file and serve a Motion to Seal identifying the documents, with a non-confidential description and a sealed in camera attachment, explaining why less drastic alternatives fail and addressing listed factors (fair trial, witness cooperation, reliance on confidentiality, public significance, harm, alternatives, and public interest including health and safety), with added family court factors; the movant bears the burden and a hearing is held. A settlement submitted for approval may not be conditioned on sealing, a settlement involving a public body may never be sealed, and every sealing order must state specific reasons; the rule does not govern private settlements dismissed under Rule 41(a)(1).","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"41.2","t":"Privacy Protection for Filings","s":"Filers must omit or redact Social Security numbers (no portion may appear), taxpayer, driver’s license, passport, and similar identifying numbers; fully redact names of minors who are sexual assault or abuse or neglect victims and otherwise use first name and last initial or initials; use only the last 4 digits of financial account numbers; give only city and state for home addresses of minors, victims, and nonparties; and give only the year of birth. A confidential reference list may be filed without a court order, responsibility to redact rests with counsel and parties (clerks do not review), the clerk may remove inflammatory filings from the public index, and anyone may make a signed written request to remove unredacted data at no charge.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; Separate Trials","s":"When actions share a common question of law or fact the court may order a joint hearing or trial or consolidate them, and for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for economy it may order separate trials of any claim or issue, always preserving the right to jury trial; the Note says this is substantially identical to the federal rule and that consolidated actions keep their separate identities.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"43","t":"Conduct of Trial","s":"Testimony is taken orally in open court, and the rule keeps SC-specific trial conduct provisions not in the federal rule: one counsel per party examines each witness (standing), opening statements may not argue and pleadings are not given to the jury, no argument on evidentiary objections unless the court asks, final argument is limited to 2 hours per side without permission, the plaintiff (or movant) opens and closes, and a motion denied by one judge may not be renewed on the same facts before another judge. Under Rule 43(k), an agreement between counsel binds only if made as a consent order or signed stipulation entered in the record, made in open court and noted on the record, or written and signed by the parties and counsel; for mediated settlements under the court-annexed ADR rules involving payment by an insurer, insurer-retained defense counsel’s signature suffices for the insured, and sealing of settlements follows Rule 41.1. Former subdivisions on examination scope, excluded evidence, and expert testimony were deleted in 1995 as now governed by the South Carolina Rules of Evidence, and affirmation may replace an oath, motions may be heard on affidavits, and the court may appoint and set pay for interpreters.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of Official Record; Foreign Law","s":"Domestic official records are proved by official publication or a custodian-attested copy with a certificate of custody, and foreign records by attested copy with a final certification (which the court may excuse for good cause); a certified statement of diligent search is evidence that no record exists, and other lawful methods remain available. A party raising foreign law must give notice in the pleadings or by other reasonable written notice, and the court may consider any relevant source, deciding the issue as a question of law.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"A subpoena must state the court, title, and civil action number and set out the text of subdivisions (c) and (d); attorneys may issue and sign subpoenas as officers of the court and the clerk issues blank signed forms, a nonparty deposition or production subpoena must issue from and be served in the county where the nonparty resides, works, or regularly does business, and a copy of any pretrial document or inspection subpoena must be served on every party at least 10 days before the compliance date and before service on the recipient. Service may be made by any non-party at least 18 years old by any Rule 4(d) or 4(j) method (including certified mail), with $25 per day and state-rate mileage tendered on arrival (not required for the State). Written objections are due within 14 days after service or before the compliance date if sooner; the court must quash a subpoena that allows unreasonable time, requires a nonparty (other than an officer, director, managing agent, or general partner of a party) to travel more than 50 miles from home county except for trial within the State, seeks privileged matter, or imposes undue burden, and may condition subpoenas seeking trade secrets, unretained expert opinions, or costly travel; unexcused noncompliance is contempt.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions Unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions to rulings are unnecessary; it is enough to make known to the court, when the ruling is made or sought, the action desired or the objection and its grounds, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced. The Note says this does not apply to exceptions to masters’ reports under Rule 53 or to appellate exceptions.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court may let counsel conduct voir dire or conduct it itself with supplemental questions, and may seat up to 6 alternate jurors, with each side getting 1 extra peremptory challenge for 1 or 2 alternates, 2 for 3 or 4, and 3 for 5 or 6, usable only against alternates. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 47(c) lets the court lodge a deliberating jury overnight, kept together under bailiff supervision and shielded from outside contact.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of Less Than Twelve; Majority Verdict","s":"The jury is 12 persons with a unanimous verdict unless the parties stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors (or fewer than 6 in magistrate’s court civil actions) or to a stated majority verdict.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"49","t":"Special Verdicts and Interrogatories","s":"The court may require only a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue (an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires) or a general verdict with written interrogatories, with rules for entering judgment, resubmitting, or ordering a new trial when answers conflict. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 49(c) requires the jury in an action to recover specific personal property to assess the property’s value and may assess actual and punitive damages for its detention.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"50","t":"Motion for a Directed Verdict and for Judgment Notwithstanding the Verdict","s":"The judge may direct a verdict when only questions of law are presented; the motion must state specific grounds and may be made at the close of an opponent’s evidence without waiving the right to present evidence. A JNOV motion, which may be joined with or alternative to a new-trial motion, must be made promptly after the jury is discharged or, in the court’s discretion, no later than 20 days after (extended from 10 days by the 2026 amendment); a timely motion stays the appeal time for all parties until receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding it, the judge keeps jurisdiction after the term ends, and a copy of a written motion must be given to the judge within 10 days after filing. Conditional new-trial rulings follow the federal pattern.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to Jury; Objection","s":"Any party may file written requests to charge at the close of evidence or earlier as directed; the court must tell counsel its proposed action on them before closing arguments but instructs the jury after arguments. Error in an instruction is preserved only by objecting before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds distinctly, with an opportunity to object outside the jury’s hearing.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the Court","s":"In bench trials and with advisory juries the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately (also for interlocutory injunctions), but findings are not needed on Rule 12, Rule 56, or other motions except Rule 41(b). A motion to amend or add findings is due within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of judgment (extended from 10 days by the 2026 amendment), stays the time to appeal for all parties until receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding it, and survives the end of the term; a copy must go to the judge within 10 days after filing, and sufficiency of the evidence may be raised on appeal without such a motion.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters and Special Referees","s":"Unique to South Carolina, the “master” is the county master-in-equity and a “special referee” is a South Carolina Bar member appointed under S.C. Code § 14-11-60; with consent, in default cases, or in foreclosures a circuit judge or the clerk may refer causes of action, and otherwise the circuit court may refer on motion or its own initiative, but any party may demand a jury under Rule 38, which returns the case to circuit court. Cases are not referred merely for a report: the master or special referee has the full powers of a circuit judge sitting without a jury and enters judgment, appeals go to the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals under the appellate rules, and special referees set their own fees subject to review if a party objects within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of the order (extended from 10 days by the 2026 amendment).","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; Costs","s":"A judgment includes any decree or order dismissing the action as to a party or finally determining a party’s rights; partial final judgments in multi-claim or multi-party cases require an express finding of no just reason for delay, and default judgments may not exceed or differ in kind from the demand. Costs go to the prevailing party as of course (against the State only as law allows) on a motion with a supporting affidavit filed within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of final judgment, or after appeal for a party first prevailing on appeal (extended from 10 days by the 2026 amendment); the clerk may tax costs on 1 day’s notice, and review by the court is on motion served within 20 days after notice. Unique to South Carolina, Rule 54(e) lists taxable costs: statutory costs and sanctions including ordered attorney’s fees, clerk’s fees, sheriff’s and comparable service fees, fees and mileage of subpoenaed witnesses who testified, and limited copy, certification, translation, map, and photograph costs.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default upon affidavit or other showing that a party failed to plead or defend, but unlike the federal rule only a judge may enter a default judgment: on affidavit or verified pleading for a liquidated or sum-certain claim against a non-appearing, competent party, and otherwise on application, with at least 3 days’ written notice to a party who has appeared, a hearing as needed, any jury right preserved, and notice of any hearing on unliquidated damages mailed first class to all defaulted parties. Attorney’s fees may be awarded without a hearing only if requested in the motion with a fee affidavit mailed to the defaulted party and no objection is filed within 10 days; special affidavit, attachment, and undertaking requirements apply after publication service on nonresidents, default may be set aside for good cause (default judgments under Rule 60(b)), and claims against the State, minors, incompetents, divorce or annulment parties, unappearing publication-served parties, and in rem defendants must be proved by evidence.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary Judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 30 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves) and a defending party at any time, with or without affidavits; the motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits at least 2 days before. Judgment is granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law, including on liability alone; the opponent may not rest on the pleadings, the court may specify uncontroverted facts in a partial ruling, may continue for discovery when affidavits are unavailable, and must award expenses and fees for bad-faith affidavits.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory Judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment actions under S.C. Code §§ 15-53-10 through 15-53-140 follow these rules, with jury trial available under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing. The Note says it matches the federal rule except for the State statutory references.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of Judgment","s":"On a general jury verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs only, or denial of all relief, the clerk promptly prepares, signs, and enters judgment unless the court orders otherwise; for other relief or special verdicts the court prepares or directs counsel to prepare the judgment for approval. Every judgment must be on a separate document and is effective only when entered, entry is not delayed for costs, and unlike the federal rule, Rule 58(b) has the clerk adjust appellate costs and record appellate judgments remitted to the trial court with cross-references.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"59","t":"New Trials; Amendment of Judgments","s":"A new trial may be granted for any reason previously recognized in South Carolina law actions (jury) or rehearings (non-jury). In jury cases the motion is made promptly after discharge or, in the court’s discretion, no later than 20 days after; in non-jury cases within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of judgment (or filing of an order disposing of the action); a motion to alter or amend under Rule 59(e) must be served within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of the order; and the court may order a new trial on its own within 20 days after entry of judgment (all extended from 10 days by the 2026 amendment). Opposing affidavits are due within 10 days, extendable by up to 20 more days; a timely motion stays the appeal time for all parties until receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding it (the Note says federal practice runs from signing), the judge keeps jurisdiction after the term ends, and a copy must go to the judge within 10 days after filing.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief From Judgment or Order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time (with appellate leave while an appeal is pending, and a copy of any written motion to the judge within 10 days after filing). Relief from a final judgment is available for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; newly discovered evidence; fraud or misconduct; a void judgment; or a satisfied, reversed, or no-longer-equitable judgment, on motion within a reasonable time and, for the first three grounds, no more than 1 year after entry; unlike the federal rule, there is no catch-all “any other reason” ground. The motion does not suspend the judgment, appellate leave is required while an appeal is pending, independent actions and relief for fraud on the court remain available, and the old writs such as coram nobis and audita querela are abolished.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless Error","s":"No error in admitting or excluding evidence or in any ruling or act is grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a verdict, judgment, or order unless refusing relief appears inconsistent with substantial justice, and the court must disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of Proceedings to Enforce a Judgment","s":"No execution or enforcement may occur until 10 days after entry of judgment, except that injunction, receivership, and accounting judgments are not stayed unless the court orders; the court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions under Rules 50, 52(b), 59, or 60 on security conditions. On appeal, a supersedeas bond obtains a stay (subject to the exceptions and the South Carolina Appellate Court Rules), effective when the bond is approved; the State and its officers and agencies need no bond, statutory stays remain available, the court may grant or modify an injunction pending appeal, and it may stay a Rule 54(b) partial judgment.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability of a Judge","s":"If a judge cannot proceed after a trial or hearing has begun but before final judgment, a successor judge is assigned and may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and finding no prejudice; in non-jury proceedings the successor must, on request, recall any available witness whose material testimony is disputed. The 2004 Note says the language is similar to federal Rule 63.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"64","t":"Remedies for Seizure; Remedies Not Provided","s":"Remedies for seizure of person or property to secure satisfaction of a judgment are available at the start of and during an action as provided by law, and where no statute or rule applies, procedures and remedies follow prior South Carolina court practice.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions; Mandamus, Habeas Corpus, and Other Remedial Writs","s":"No temporary injunction issues without notice; a temporary restraining order without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury, must be endorsed with date and hour, served with the summons and complaint if not yet served, and expires within the time fixed, not exceeding 10 days, unless extended once for good cause or by consent; the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice, and a TRO may not suspend a business’s ordinary operations. Security is required except in divorce, child custody, and non-support actions (where it is discretionary) and from the State; orders must state reasons and describe the restrained acts specifically. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 65(f) governs mandamus, habeas corpus, and other remedial writs, which require notice, a supporting affidavit or verified complaint, and service with the summons and complaint, and allows joinder of damages claims and costs and fees to the prevailing party.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order, and administration by receivers follows State law. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 66(b) gives every receiver of a debtor’s property, unless restricted, general power to sue for and collect the debtor’s debts and rents, compromise doubtful claims, and sue or defend in the debtor’s name.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in Court","s":"In an action seeking money or a deliverable thing, any party may, on notice to all parties and with leave of court, deposit all or part of the money or thing with the court; money is placed in a bank authorized to receive public funds and withdrawn only by the clerk’s check as the court directs.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of Judgment","s":"The Note says this rule follows the State statutes and is not identical to the federal rule: any party except in domestic relations actions may file a written offer of judgment no later than 20 days before trial, and the offeree may file a written acceptance within 20 days after service or at least 10 days before trial, whichever is earlier, after which the court issues judgment. An offer not accepted by then is rejected (a counteroffer is not a rejection), may be withdrawn before acceptance, and may be superseded by a later offer. If the offeror obtains a result at least as favorable, the offeror recovers court costs from the date of the offer, and a plaintiff-offeror recovers 8% interest on the verdict from the offer date to judgment while a defendant-offeror gets the award reduced by 8% interest for that period; contractual attorney’s fee rights are not affected.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, with execution and supplementary proceedings as provided by State law, and the judgment creditor may examine any person, including the debtor, using the discovery rules.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for Specific Acts; Vesting Title","s":"If a party fails to perform a judgment directing a conveyance, delivery of documents, or another specific act, the court may have it done by an appointee at that party’s cost, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or for property in the State enter a judgment divesting and vesting title; a party awarded possession may get a writ of execution or assistance from the clerk.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"71","t":"Foreclosure and Partition","s":"Unique to South Carolina, foreclosure and partition actions are tried by the court and ordinarily referred to a master, who computes each claimant’s total debt (principal, interest to the hearing and the pre- and post-judgment rates, escrow items, costs, and attorney’s fees through conclusion), may set lien priorities, confirms lis pendens compliance, and keeps a record of hearings. The judgment orders a sale by the master or other officer with a legal description, advertisement, time and place, senior liens, and the required good-faith deposit; unless a deficiency judgment is waived in the pleadings or in writing, bidding stays open until the 30th day after the sale, excluding the sale day. Surplus claims must be verified and filed within 45 days after the statement of receipts and disbursements or are waived; partition requires joinder of a decedent’s representative, heirs, and encumbrancers, bars partial partition of commonly owned tracts without all cotenants’ consent, allocates fees from a common fund only to the extent of common benefit, and may use a writ to 5 commissioners who act within 1 month to divide in kind, recommend allotment for payment, or recommend sale (or the court may proceed without a writ).","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"71.1","t":"Post-Conviction Relief Actions","s":"Unique to South Carolina (the Note says there is no federal counterpart), post-conviction relief follows the Uniform Post-Conviction Procedure Act, S.C. Code §§ 17-27-10 to -120, with these rules applying where not inconsistent. An application may not be filed while a direct appeal is pending or the time to perfect one has not run, must use the prescribed form, and is a separate civil action captioned applicant v. State of South Carolina; the court appoints counsel for an indigent applicant when a hearing is required, the applicant must prove entitlement by a preponderance of the evidence, the clerk gives notice of the final order under Rule 77(d), and appellate review follows Rule 243, SCACR, with counsel continuing on appeal unless relieved (Rule 12(a) separately gives the State 60 days to respond for guilty pleas and 90 days for trials).","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"72","t":"Judgments and Orders Subject to Appeal","s":"An appeal may be taken, as provided by law, from any final judgment or appealable order; the 1989 Note says the rule does not change existing appellate jurisdiction law, such as S.C. Code § 14-3-330.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"73","t":"Procedure on Appeal to Supreme Court or Court of Appeals","s":"Appeals to the South Carolina Supreme Court or Court of Appeals follow the South Carolina Appellate Court Rules (SCACR), not these rules.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"74","t":"Procedure on Appeal to the Circuit Court","s":"Unique to South Carolina, appeals to circuit court from inferior courts and administrative agencies follow the governing statutes except that the notice of appeal must be served on all parties within 30 days after receipt of written notice of the judgment, order, or decision; the notice of intention to appeal is filed with the circuit clerk and the lower tribunal within the statutory time or, if none, this rule’s time, and the circuit court proceeds under these rules with statutory priority.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"75","t":"Record on Appeal to the Circuit Court; Transmittal","s":"Appeals to circuit court are heard on the original record, which the lower court’s or agency’s clerk (or its judge or chief official if there is no clerk) must certify and transmit within 30 days after the notice of appeal is filed, extendable for good cause; the circuit clerk then notifies the parties in writing that the record has been filed.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"76","t":"Arbitration; Automobile Reparation","s":"Unique to South Carolina, resident circuit judges designate panels of arbitrators for actions under the Automobile Reparation Reform Act of 1974, made up of all attorneys in good standing actively practicing with principal offices in the judicial circuit. Depositions follow Rules 26 through 32 but the Rule 30(a)(2) monetary limit does not apply, and the clerk keeps an arbitration docket in the form specified by the Director of South Carolina Court Administration.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"77","t":"Courts and Clerks","s":"Courts are always open for filing papers, issuing process, and making interlocutory motions and orders; trials on the merits are held in open court, other matters may be handled in chambers anywhere, but no hearing other than ex parte or post-trial motions may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk’s office is open business hours except Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, and the clerk may grant process, defaults, and other routine applications subject to court review. On entry of an order or judgment the clerk must immediately serve notice by first-class mail on each non-defaulted party (or by Notice of Electronic Filing for SCE-File participants), which is sufficient notice for all purposes, with special service rules for post-conviction relief; terms of court may not be cancelled or added without the Chief Justice’s prior approval.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"78","t":"Clerks of Court; Records, Abstracts, Books","s":"Subject to Supreme Court approval, the Office of Court Administration sets procedures for creating, maintaining, and destroying records of the clerks and other courts and for court statistics, with noncompliance punishable as contempt. The clerk enters in the Abstracts of Judgments book every final judgment or order affecting title to or liens on property, keeps indices and other required books, and produces the Code, rules, calendars, and file book on the court’s request.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"79","t":"Calendar of Civil Actions; File Book","s":"Unique to South Carolina, each county clerk keeps one cross-indexed File Book of all circuit civil actions and appeals from inferior courts and agencies, assigns civil action numbers under the Supreme Court’s approved system, and keeps a motion calendar; the plaintiff must state at filing whether the case is jury or non-jury. No action may be heard until entered in the file book with all pleadings filed, only a judge or the clerk may make entries, and no action may be marked ended unless there is a Rule 41 notice or stipulation, a consent order signed by the judge or clerk, or a court-ordered dismissal; noncompliance is punishable as contempt.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographic Report of Transcript as Evidence","s":"When reported testimony from a prior trial or hearing is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a copy of the transcript certified by the reporter or by the clerk who has custody of the original.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability","s":"These rules apply to every trial court of civil jurisdiction in South Carolina within its legal powers, and as far as practicable in magistrate’s, probate, and family courts where not inconsistent with their statutes and rules; where neither statute nor these rules provide, prior State practice governs.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and Venue","s":"The rules do not extend or limit any court’s jurisdiction or venue, except that an action filed in the wrong county or court must be transferred to a proper one rather than dismissed. No order accepting or directing removal is required when a petition to remove a case to federal court is filed. (The text contains no Rule 83.)","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The Supreme Court prescribes the content and format of forms required by these rules, and use of the prescribed forms is mandatory.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"85","t":"Title; Criminal Procedure; Rules Repealed","s":"The rules are cited as “Rule __, SCRCP.” Former Circuit Court criminal practice rules 4, 25, 35, 61, 72, 95, 98, 103, 104, and 105 were renumbered Criminal Practice Rules 1 through 10 (an Editor’s Note says that appendix was later repealed by Rule 39, SCRCrimP), and all other prior Circuit Court practice rules were repealed.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective Date","s":"The rules took effect July 1, 1985, governing new actions and further proceedings in pending ones unless applying them would be infeasible or unjust in a particular pending case, and the 1986 amendments took effect May 1, 1986.","u":"https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/civil/"}]});
